00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:02.760
- Thank you, Gary, and thank you, Roy.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.130
I'm excited to be here.

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:06.160
- Jenny, I'm actually going
to use your prior experience

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.180
a little bit here to bring value,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.970
because I'm reading
Twitter and trying to bring

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.540
as much value to everybody.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:11.920
Hashtag Marketing For the Now if you have

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.720
a question or an observation.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.100
Jenny, talk to me about audio.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.530
Obviously, you know, you being at Gimlet,

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.910
and a pioneer there, just two seconds

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:22.050
before we go into other matters.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.610
How do you see audio as a, as a medium?

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:26.850
Where do you see it sitting?

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:30.210
Obviously, you know, Roy's
career a lot, you know,

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.390
was built on the back of radio.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.370
We see this whole
revolution of podcasting.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.130
Your two cents on it,
because I do think you have

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:37.993
a lot of important observations.

00:01:37.993 --> 00:01:42.993
- Yeah, I mean, audio, I
specifically took the job at Gimlet

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.600
in order to learn audio,
because I felt like it was

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.860
an extremely important
touchpoint that was missed

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.500
during the day, from that
I used to call it the,

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.270
well I still call it the
idea of the ear strategy,

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.270
meaning once they leave the
couch, how do you actually keep

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.853
people engaged in your brand?

00:01:56.914 --> 00:02:00.440
And with Alexa, and with
cars, it's just makes sense

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.050
that you continue the
story throughout audio,

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.170
or whether that's in your ears.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.350
And it's also what I learned there is

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.090
just how intimate, really,
the audio experience is.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.620
And I, Daniel X said something
really, I think important

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.130
when he acquired, or his
company acquired Gimlet,

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.120
obviously that just showed
the, I think, relevance

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.060
of where they were putting their
bets in regards to Spotify,

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.600
embedding in podcasts, and
also just audio storytelling.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.630
And he said, "I can't believe
that the ear or the eye is

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.830
ten times more expensive
to buy than the ear."

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:32.880
And he just felt like that
was just something to say

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.930
this is actually an
extremely important medium.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:39.420
And so going to there
has actually helped me

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.590
as another step in my
career to realize, okay,

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.890
let's just use an
example for Blue's Clues,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.890
and maybe your son's too old for that,

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.180
but Blues Clues, we're going to do

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.270
Story Time with Josh and
Blue, whereas you could use it

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:54.272
at nap time, or you
could use it at bedtime.

00:02:54.272 --> 00:02:55.970
And so just trying to find
these moments or like, okay,

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:59.350
the average drive is eight minutes to,

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.970
you know, to a preschool.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.702
What do I do with the kid in the car?

00:03:03.702 --> 00:03:05.290
And okay, I've got them
listening to Story Pirates,

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:06.990
because it's actually an audio experience.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.500
And then also I just think that parents,

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.270
including myself with a six year old,

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:13.390
especially during this
time, is really trying

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.410
to figure out, "How do I
get my kid off the iPad?"

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.980
And if I can get them into more

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.823
of imagination through sound,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.043
and actually creating worlds,
just through listening.

00:03:24.043 --> 00:03:25.490
- I think that's a big deal.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.160
- Yeah, it's so anyway, that
was longer than my two cents

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.690
or two seconds, but I
do, I think audio plays-

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:32.627
- No, no, but this is
why we wanted you on,

00:03:32.627 --> 00:03:34.440
and this is why I wanted that question.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.870
I think your point, when
you, when you listen to some

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:40.857
of the incredible storytellers
of the 1970s, 1960s,

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:46.350
a lot of them referred to
sitting around the radio

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:50.590
and listening pre-TV
as an incredible driver

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:52.340
for their capacity for imagination.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.253
And I think why not replicate that?

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:56.340
I like that.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.840
Roy?

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.300
- When you all, let's
just talk about the pivot

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:04.280
at Nickelodeon, the
cultural pivot, not COVID,

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.939
but with George Floyd and
just totally as a company.

00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:10.690
What were some of the internal
discussions that you all had

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:12.360
as you started making
the decision over it?

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.340
Because I feel like the
corporations and networks,

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.150
there was kind of a
staggering of which companies

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:19.710
and which media started doing stuff,

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:22.270
and I felt like Nickelodeon
was at the front of that.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.380
What was, where was the decision?

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:25.970
Talk me through the decision to be one

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:27.920
of the first networks to
make a bold statement.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.770
And you can tell everybody
what the statement was

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:31.400
with the eight forty six.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.880
- Yeah, so it wasn't hard at all.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.750
And that was, I think
we realized very quickly

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.460
what side we wanted to be on.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:41.100
And Viacom, CBS came out
with, we're going to go dark.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.310
Bob came out, we're going to go dark

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:43.750
for eight minutes and 46 seconds.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:45.730
And we said, "Who is participating?"

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.140
And I have to give credit to my boss,

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.950
Brian Robbins didn't waste one second

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:53.080
and said, "Every single
network including Nick Jr."

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.960
We did want to make a statement

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.000
in regards to where our support lies.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.010
We also truly, truly
believe that education

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.650
and enlightenment starts
at a very young age,

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.970
and that we have the platform
to help start education,

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:10.880
and actually foster those
difficult conversations,

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.130
but also help educate and enlighten

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:14.360
is the word that we like to use,

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.470
so not just entertained,
but also enlighten.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.480
And so it was a very
simple decision to go dark

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.060
for eight minutes and forty six seconds.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.700
And so there was a couple
of different spots.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.858
There was a George Floyd, eight minutes

00:05:26.858 --> 00:05:28.160
and forty six seconds of a man breathing,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.568
a very, very provocative that caused a lot

00:05:30.568 --> 00:05:35.380
of conversation I will say,
on on Twitter and social

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.690
just about how to talk to children.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:40.580
And we also ran a Kid's Bill
of Rights for eight minutes

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.880
and forty six seconds, so "You
have the right to be heard.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.120
You have the right to not be judged

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.350
by the color of your skin.

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:46.890
You have the right to an education."

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:48.690
That's turned into a much larger campaign

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.400
that we're running, but
that also, so there was,

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:53.838
it was a very easy decision for us.

00:05:53.838 --> 00:05:57.070
And, you know, we believe, you
know, all the conversation,

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:58.200
you know, really opened up to say,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.800
"Listen, you know, we can actually talk

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.620
to out kids about more difficult things.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.850
And it allowed us to say,
"Listen, we're treating

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:05.480
our children, like, with kid gloves."

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.590
And we're also treating
our children, you know,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:08.767
some people have different experience,

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:10.740
and some kids live in fear every day,

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.270
and we owe it to ourselves
for white children,

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.410
for black children, to
open this conversation,

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.830
because racism starts between
the ages of two and four,

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:19.700
and is embedded by age twelve."

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.760
And I think that we have
them in the sweet spot,

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.240
two to two to eleven.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.560
And so we cannot not
help in this situation.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.060
And thus, I think then
moving on to the Juneteenth

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.460
education that we did on
that Friday, the 19th.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.180
And then moving to Alicia Keys,

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.520
we did another town hall
for Race, Kids and Unity.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.450
That again, you could watch
on YouTube if you'd like,

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:43.240
it's really incredible, some
schools have picked it up

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.960
to sort of teach and help
start the conversation,

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.720
but it was not hard for us at all.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.450
And we're continuing to,
you know, I've now made this

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:52.960
an initiative at
Nickelodeon where, you know,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.150
we're putting money behind it,

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:58.010
and I'm working with Maverick
Carter, LeBron's company,

00:06:58.010 --> 00:06:59.890
and we're hopefully working with them.

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.090
We're coming up with ways
we could work together

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.450
to develop content, whether
short form, long form,

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:05.860
that this is not just a campaign.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:09.390
It's a sustained effort
that we continue to do,

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:12.325
both inside the company
and outside the company,

00:07:12.325 --> 00:07:14.930
meaning diversify our employees
also, as well as our vendors

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:17.420
and our outside, behind the
camera, in front of the camera.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:19.866
It's become a huge initiative for us,

00:07:19.866 --> 00:07:21.323
and we are definitely not slowing down.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:26.830
- What's the biggest
challenge for, you know,

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:30.190
you and your executive
team during COVID itself.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:33.100
When it first hit, were you surprised

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.370
by any of the remote capabilities,

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:39.490
or did you feel like that
was going to work out?

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:40.710
You have obviously worked

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:43.350
in some progressive companies in the past.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:46.810
Did you yourself, are you missing like,

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.250
random interactions in
the office and travel,

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:51.105
or are you loving this?

00:07:51.105 --> 00:07:52.410
Like, little bit of just like the insights

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.940
to leaders during this time.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.470
- I think it's incredible how people have

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:01.050
creatively pivoted and
sort of embraced and solved

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.930
a lot of the creative
adaptability if you will.

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:05.900
So, yes, I think we all miss a little bit

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.590
of those hallway conversations,
and we miss being able to,

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:12.675
you know, basically just knock on,

00:08:12.675 --> 00:08:14.540
walk in and talk to somebody.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:16.920
But I think what's been really great is

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.450
we've been able to figure
out how to do our work,

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:22.300
even if it comes down to shoots,

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.680
like, working with families
to shoot themselves.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.480
And I think what we've,
you know, I think Gary,

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.700
you and I talked a little
bit about this as sort of,

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:30.210
you know, the humanistic approach.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.400
With the stuff we're doing
now is a lot less polished,

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.910
and actually it's working better for us,

00:08:33.910 --> 00:08:35.600
because it's more human and it's more

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.520
about actually families and
them interacting together.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:40.150
So I think that's been a really positive.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:42.480
I'll say the hardest thing
for us is that not being

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.150
in production with new
shows, so we're coming up,

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:46.610
we're not able to go on set.

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:50.470
So I think just keeping
the content live alive,

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:53.979
meaning that we're coming up
with shows like Unfiltered,

00:08:53.979 --> 00:08:57.160
which is sort of a game show
that we do completely remotely.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.550
We have these Danger Force shorts

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.870
we're doing completely remote.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.240
Our marketing team is
now developing shows.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.550
We developed a SpongeBob
Fan Favorite Special

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.343
with the cast rereading.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:09.973
You should go check it
out, it's really fun.

00:09:09.973 --> 00:09:14.650
They're, it's on YouTube,
but they're acting out

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:16.930
famous scenes, and it's really great.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.930
So we're just coming up
with kind of creative ideas

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:20.900
of what programming could
be, because this might last

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:22.270
for a really long time.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.892
Animation, obviously-

00:09:23.892 --> 00:09:24.810
- How long-

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:26.240
Yeah, animation's in a better spot

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:27.860
than live action or filming.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:30.310
Do you feel like this is,

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:33.160
like, what's your personal,
like, any insights to that?

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:35.260
I mean, are we looking
at, I mean, at this point,

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:38.590
it feels like the rest of
this year is definitely not

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:40.450
going to be the normal
we knew prior to this.

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:43.310
Is there programming concerns?

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:44.500
Or because I was going to just ask that,

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:46.960
your animation shops, do
you feel like you're sitting

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.040
in a little bit of a better spot

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:49.740
than maybe some of your contemporaries?

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.690
- I think we are sitting,
I mean, animation takes

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.380
a really long time to do,
so we're having to speed up

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:56.590
that process a little bit,
but it's really great.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.720
We have some amazing
shows, like Casa Grandes,

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:00.800
The Loud House, we have New SpongeBob,

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.983
we have It's Pony we just renewed.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.120
We have also just, you
know, green lit a bunch

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.260
of other things that will
happen come next summer.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.180
So we're, I think we're in
a very good place there.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:11.390
And I think we're also
in a very good place

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:14.610
that we've got an incredible
YouTube team that comes

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:17.070
from Fullscreen run by Ashley Kaplan,

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.670
that actually can make incredible content

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:20.900
remotely really well.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:22.450
So I think we're in a really good spot,

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.300
but I would say that for
the rest of this year,

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.170
it is going to be challenging, you know,

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:29.010
especially with what's
happening in California

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:30.840
how it seems to be getting
worse at the moment.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.120
But we're cautiously
optimistic that we'll get

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.970
a few things in, and
we're shooting some things

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:37.250
in Vancouver, which is in a better space.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:39.850
So I think we're definitely
going to have a good Q4.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:41.870
And so that's, that's the thing.

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:43.670
I think some people
that are able to shoot,

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:45.710
let's say, in Europe,
might be at a better place,

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:47.430
but if you can shoot in Canada-

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:48.730
- Right, right, right,
right, right, right,

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:50.190
that makes a lot of sense.

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:51.077
Roy?

00:10:51.077 --> 00:10:54.330
- As a parent, how much does that,

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:56.493
how much does it help you at your job?

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:59.140
- Oh, immensely.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.443
Another reason I say I take
each job I take for a reason,

00:11:01.443 --> 00:11:03.140
when I took audio, I
wanted to learn audio.

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:05.720
I took this job, one,
because I wanted to see if-

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:06.850
well, one, I love Brian Robbins.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:07.990
I think he's an incredible leader,

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:10.420
and I always wanted to work for him.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.890
And I also just felt
being six years, sort of

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:14.530
on the digital side, what could I bring

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:17.210
to a linear channel that's
evolving into digital

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:19.600
to help it make that move?

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:22.280
But it's also, I would be
lying if I said it doesn't,

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:24.830
I can't, I love it that I'm a hero at my,

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:28.563
that my six year old kid loves what I do,

00:11:28.563 --> 00:11:30.580
and also loves the stuff I work on.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:33.030
But I also have a focus group of one here

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.010
that I can bounce things off him,

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:36.850
and I run trailers behind him and say,

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:39.270
you know, in front of him,
and so it's really incredible.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.270
And also it's really
helped to open dialogue.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:43.563
You know, the thing we
were talking about before

00:11:43.563 --> 00:11:46.240
with George Florida and racism
about how I talk to my child.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:47.940
And it's helped figure
out, like, how do I talk

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.180
to a kindergartner about
racism, and he's going to be

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:54.240
in first grade, and how do I
talk to like, a sixth grader?

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:55.230
And it's very different.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:56.687
And so we're finding
these ways to figure out,

00:11:56.687 --> 00:11:58.640
"How do I make the most
impact with each age?"

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.840
And so I do have one
focus group of one here

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:03.253
that's six that I can
bounce some things off of.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.190
